# Label Preview (4x6) – UI Implementation Rule

## Objective
Provide a client-rendered, print-ready 4x6 shipping label preview, accessible from the purchase success screen, using only mocked data and following the project’s naming and accessibility standards.

## Technology & Design
- Framework: Next.js App Router (client component)
- Language: TypeScript
- UI: Tailwind CSS + shadcn/ui Button
- Accessibility: Keyboard + ARIA; controls hidden in print
- Naming & Scoping: Follow `@naming-and-scoping.mdc` strictly
- Animations: Minimal; avoid layout shift

## Route & Params
- Path: `/label/preview`
- Param: `tracking` (query)
- Default tracking fallback: `PCG-TEST-000001`

## Requirements
- Print-ready canvas sized exactly 4x6 inches on paper
- On-screen preview centered with subtle container styling
- Elements:
  - Header with brand and tracking
  - From/To blocks
  - Service badge + ETA line (mock)
  - Barcode (mock deterministic bars by tracking)
  - QR placeholder
  - Footer notes
- Controls:
  - Back, View Tracking, Print
  - Hide all controls in print view
- Success screen integration: “Open Label Preview” button routing with tracking number

## Acceptance Criteria
- Page loads with or without `tracking`; fallback applied without errors
- Print shows only the label content; no extra UI
- All interactive controls have accessible names and keyboard support
- All IDs unique and prefixed with `parcego-`
- No backend calls; fully mocked content

## Naming
- IDs: `parcego-label-preview-container`, `parcego-label-tracking`, `parcego-label-barcode`, `parcego-label-qr`, `parcego-label-preview-print-btn`, `parcego-label-open-preview-btn`
- Classes: `parcego-label__barcode`, `parcego-label__meta` (as needed), alongside Tailwind utility classes

## Out of Scope
- Real barcode/QR generation
- PDF generation
- Live merchant or recipient lookup

